==Old English==

===Etymology===
The original sense may have been a poetical comparison with a wild animal: it seems to be cognate with Old Norse {{Latinx|''[[bjǫrn]]''}} ‘bear’, from Germanic ''*bernu-z''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/beorn/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
'''beorn''' {{m}} (''plural'' '''[[beornas]]''')

# {{poetic| lang=ang}} [[man]], [[warrior]]
#: ''se beorn on waruþe scip gemette'': the man found a ship on the strand.  (''Legend of St Andrew'')

